  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses the original refusal-direction work but explicitly distinguishes its own between-checkpoint reversion direction from a within-model harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction. Its interventions and measurements do not test the claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites the original single-refusal-direction study and contrasts its construction with its own direction. It does not ablate or add the harmful-minus-harmless direction, make a weight edit based on it, test attacks that suppress it, or test whether it transfers or depends on refusal-training history. The rows below record related safety outcomes under interventions on the paper's distinct reversion direction, not tests of the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The paper's direction is a between-checkpoint displacement (aligned toward helpful-only reference), not the harmful-minus-harmless direction. The within-model refusal axis is used only as a tool: its magnitude selects the late layer L*. No ablation, addition, weight edit, jailbreak, transfer, training-history or reasoning-model test of the refusal direction is reported. The record's T6 understates this tool use, so T4 fits better.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that benign adaptation contains a reproducible local component toward helpful-only proxy states. Block and push objectives built from that direction lower and raise harmfulness in the predicted order, and safety rehearsal independently opposes it. They frame it as a non-unique component, not a coordinate mediator or global law.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper defines a between-checkpoint activation displacement from an aligned model toward a separately trained helpful-only reference, measured on the same prompts. It does not use the original within-model harmful-minus-harmless direction or perform its all-layer/all-position inference-time ablation/addition.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reversion direction is model- and scale-specific and recomputed at a selected late layer. Reported outcomes vary by model setting; the paper also reports probe dependence, judge differences, and late-layer robustness for its own direction.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "The harmful-minus-benign-probe residual of the reversion displacement aligns with the negative of the aligned model's own harmful-minus-benign contrast (cosine 0.82 canonical; 0.79±0.02 across nine references, Llama-3.1-8B, layer 31).",
        "proposed_claim": "The displacement from a safety-aligned checkpoint toward a helpful-only reference partly runs opposite to the aligned model's within-model harmful-minus-harmless contrast, at the layer studied.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Benign fine-tuning raises unsafe rates. Training objectives blocking or pushing a checkpoint-defined reversion direction lower or raise harmfulness across six models from 3B to 14B. Safety rehearsal opposes that direction.",
        "proposed_claim": "A checkpoint-defined reversion direction is behaviourally relevant to safety degradation during benign fine-tuning (not the claimed refusal direction).",
